// The SECOND shape of vacuity: a permit that always matches and never grants.
//
// The first shape is a gate whose condition no session can satisfy -- approval_gate_response.dw,
// where the approval it waits on is denied and so recorded as `::error` rather than `::response`.
// This one is different and a condition-satisfiability check would miss it entirely: the permit's
// condition is `true`, it matches every trade, and it still authorizes nothing, because Cedar's
// forbid-overrides-permit rule is what decides. Dogwood keeps that rule.
//
// Both shapes matter because both look like working policy to a reader, and neither is reported by
// a syntax check or a validator. `tests/strands/vacuity.py` separates matched from GRANTED, which
// is what lets it see this one.
//
// Contrived on purpose -- nobody writes these two lines together. In a real policy set the forbid
// arrives later, from a different author, and covers more than they realised.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);

forbid (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);
